#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# hue

#% text
# `hue(h)` is the fully saturated, fully bright colour of hue h: 0 red, 1/3 green, 2/3 blue, 1 red again.

#% text
# `hue(h, s, b)` is the colour with hue h, saturation s and brightness b, each between 0 and 1.

#% text
# `hue(h, s, b, a)` is that colour with opacity a.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The hue is cyclic: only its fractional part counts, so `hue(1.25)` is `hue(0.25)`.

#% item
# A saturation of 0 gives a gray of the given brightness; a brightness of 0 is black.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# Pure green as red, green and blue components:

#% cell
color_convert(hue(1/3), rgb_color)
#% output
#= rgb_color(0.0, 1.0, 0.0)

#% text
# A row of disks around the colour wheel:

#% cell
graphics(table([hue(k/6), disk([k, 0], 0.4)], k, range(0, 5)))
#% output
#= image("hue.files/hue-2.png")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Paler and darker versions of one hue:

#% cell
[color_convert(hue(0.6, 0.3, 1), rgb_color), color_convert(hue(0.6, 1, 0.5), rgb_color)]
#% output
#= [rgb_color(0.7, 0.8200000000000001, 1.0), rgb_color(0.0, 0.20000000000000018, 0.5)]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `rgb_color`, `gray_level`, `color_convert`, `blend`
